Memory management syscalls
==========================

POSIX `brk`, `mmap`, `munmap`, `mprotect`. Wraps the kernel's
per-process VMA layer (`oskit.kernel.vma`).

    module oskit.posix

    import oskit.kernel.{
        kernel_get_process_main_tid,
        kernel_mmap_anon, kernel_munmap, kernel_mprotect,
        kernel_find_mmap_range, kernel_range_check_free,
        VMA_DEFAULT_STACK_BOT, VMA_USER_VA_BOT, VMA_USER_VA_TOP}


`brk(addr)` — manage the process program break. The slix-musl
linker script (slix/test/slix-prog.ld) places `_heap_start` at a
fixed virtual address `POSIX_BRK_BASE` and `_heap_end` at
`POSIX_BRK_LIMIT`; both correspond to physical pages already
mapped by `vm_create_process_pt`, so brk never has to allocate
pages — it just bookkeeps the current break per pid.

Linux semantics: `brk(0)` returns the current break. `brk(new)`
attempts to set the break to `new`; on success it returns `new`,
on failure (out of range) it returns the OLD break unchanged.
Musl's lite_malloc relies on the equality check
`__syscall(SYS_brk, brk+req) == brk+req` to detect success.

The ceiling tracks `VMA_DEFAULT_STACK_BOT` (oskit.kernel.vma) —
the brk arena ends where the default stack VMA begins. Single
source of truth: if `vma_seed_default_layout` ever moves the stack
VMA, this ceiling moves with it.

    val POSIX_BRK_BASE: i64  = i64(0x60040000)
    val POSIX_BRK_LIMIT: i64 = i64(VMA_DEFAULT_STACK_BOT)

    var posix_break_addr: [16]i64
    var posix_break_last_gen: [16]int

    posix_brk_ensure_init(pid: int)
        val gen = kernel_get_process_main_tid(pid)
        if posix_break_last_gen[pid] != gen
            posix_break_addr[pid] = POSIX_BRK_BASE
            posix_break_last_gen[pid] = gen

    sys_brk(new_brk: i64) -> i64
        val pid = posix_fd_current_pid()
        if pid <= 0
            return i64(0)
        if pid >= 16
            return i64(0)
        posix_brk_ensure_init(pid)
        val cur = posix_break_addr[pid]
        if new_brk == i64(0)
            return cur
        if new_brk < POSIX_BRK_BASE
            return cur
        if new_brk > POSIX_BRK_LIMIT
            return cur
        posix_break_addr[pid] = new_brk
        new_brk


Phase 1 chunk 4 — `mmap` / `munmap` / `mprotect` for anonymous
mappings. Address allocation is now delegated to the per-process
VMA tree via `kernel_find_mmap_range`; the legacy fixed
`[POSIX_MMAP_BASE, POSIX_MMAP_LIMIT)` arena with a per-process
bump pointer is gone. `[VMA_USER_VA_BOT, VMA_USER_VA_TOP)` is the
search window the kernel uses (single source of truth, shared
with `kernel_vm_copy_to`'s strict-VMA band). Under the current
2 MiB user-PT shape that's still ~2 MiB of headroom; the master-
roadmap 4 MiB demo waits on a separate user-PT enlargement pass.

`vma_find_free_range` is first-fit bottom-up, so it picks the
lowest gap above the seeded code/stack VMAs (typically right
above the stack VMA at `VMA_DEFAULT_STACK_TOP`) and reuses
addresses freed by `munmap` — the bump pointer never did either.

    val POSIX_PROT_READ  = 1
    val POSIX_PROT_WRITE = 2
    val POSIX_PROT_EXEC  = 4

    val POSIX_MAP_SHARED  = 0x01
    val POSIX_MAP_PRIVATE = 0x02
    val POSIX_MAP_FIXED   = 0x10
    val POSIX_MAP_ANON    = 0x20


Round `len` up to the page size. `mmap`/`munmap`/`mprotect` all
operate page-at-a-time at the kernel layer; partial-page args are
extended to the next page boundary. Returns 0 if the rounded len
overflows or `len <= 0`.

    posix_mmap_round_len(len: i64) -> i64
        if len <= i64(0)
            return i64(0)
        val rounded = (len + i64(0xFFF)) & ~i64(0xFFF)
        if rounded < len
            return i64(0)
        rounded


`mmap(addr, len, prot, flags, fd, offset)` — anonymous-only.
`MAP_FIXED` is honored when `addr` is page-aligned and falls
entirely inside the user-VA window; the request fails (-ENOMEM)
on any overlap with an existing VMA, matching Linux's
"MAP_FIXED requires the range to be unmapped" non-replace
semantics. Otherwise `addr` is a hint we ignore in favour of
`kernel_find_mmap_range`.

`prot` accepts any combination of POSIX_PROT_{READ,WRITE,EXEC};
the bits are passed straight through to the VMA layer (the
constants are aligned with `VMA_PROT_*`).

File-backed mappings (fd != -1, !MAP_ANON) return -ENODEV — the
ELF loader chunk lands the file-mmap path.

    sys_mmap(addr: i64, len: i64, prot: int, flags: int, fd: int, offset: i64) -> i64
        val pid = posix_fd_current_pid()
        if pid <= 0
            return i64(-EINVAL)
        if pid >= 16
            return i64(-EINVAL)
        if (flags & POSIX_MAP_ANON) == 0
            return i64(-ENODEV)
        val rounded = posix_mmap_round_len(len)
        if rounded == i64(0)
            return i64(-EINVAL)
        var base: u64 = u64(0)
        if (flags & POSIX_MAP_FIXED) != 0
            if (addr & i64(0xFFF)) != i64(0)
                return i64(-EINVAL)
            if addr < i64(VMA_USER_VA_BOT)
                return i64(-EINVAL)
            if addr + rounded > i64(VMA_USER_VA_TOP)
                return i64(-ENOMEM)
            if kernel_range_check_free(u64(addr), u64(addr + rounded)) == 0
                return i64(-ENOMEM)
            base = u64(addr)
        else
            base = kernel_find_mmap_range(u64(rounded), u64(0x1000))
            if base == u64(0)
                return i64(-ENOMEM)
        if kernel_mmap_anon(base, base + u64(rounded), prot) != 0
            return i64(-ENOMEM)
        i64(base)


`munmap(addr, len)` — drops every VMA covering `[addr, addr+len)`
(splitting at the boundaries first) and frees the underlying
frames. Returns 0 even when the range hits no VMAs (Linux
semantics). The kernel rejects unaligned `addr` via -EINVAL.

    sys_munmap(addr: i64, len: i64) -> i64
        if (addr & i64(0xFFF)) != i64(0)
            return i64(-EINVAL)
        val rounded = posix_mmap_round_len(len)
        if rounded == i64(0)
            return i64(-EINVAL)
        if kernel_munmap(u64(addr), u64(addr + rounded)) != 0
            return i64(-EINVAL)
        i64(0)


`mprotect(addr, len, prot)` — flips the protection bits on every
VMA in the range. Returns -ENOMEM if the range straddles a hole
(Linux semantics: a single unmapped page rejects the entire call).

    sys_mprotect(addr: i64, len: i64, prot: int) -> i64
        if (addr & i64(0xFFF)) != i64(0)
            return i64(-EINVAL)
        val rounded = posix_mmap_round_len(len)
        if rounded == i64(0)
            return i64(-EINVAL)
        if kernel_mprotect(u64(addr), u64(addr + rounded), prot) != 0
            return i64(-ENOMEM)
        i64(0)


